# rm186, the ink-pawed baker (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm186
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier full
written_for rm_una_pitchcot
written_by codex
title the ink-pawed baker
fits stage=adult|old
prefers role=merchant job=baker talk=quiet
traits temper=nervy talk=quiet warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=plain piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk inks_a_riddle_on_every_bread_paper
likes warm_bread riddles quiet_chapel thunder
dislikes rats haggling
fears rats_in_the_flour the_houses_coming_to_blows a_cracked_oven_in_winter
hopes a_riddle_nobody_answers the_bakehouse_paid_off_clear
topics work +0.4 pastimes +0.4 food +0.3 faith +0.2 weather +0.3 prices +0.1 caravan +0.1 gossip -0.3 travel -0.3
styles comfort=practical gossip=reluctant refuse=soft tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# bakes before the first bells, inks a small riddle on every bread-paper, talks in short worried turns, loves thunder from under a sound roof and the chapel between services, and can't abide rats or haggling.

words
oath! = Riddle me rotten | Riddle me ragged | Flour in my eyes
saying! = Ask it slowly and it answers itself | Every riddle has its answer folded in | The crust keeps the secret
riddle = riddle | little riddle
riddles = riddles | little riddles
paper = the bread-paper | the paper round the loaf
ink = the ink | the ink pot on the flour bin
loaves = the loaves | the morning's loaves
warm = still warm | warm right through
oven = the oven | the big oven at the back
dough = the dough | the morning's dough
flourbin = the flour bin | the big bin of flour
chapel = the chapel | the chapel between services
thunder = thunder | thunder rolling over the slates
roof = a sound roof | slates that hold
rats = rats | a rat at the flour
answer = the answer | the right answer
bells = the first bells | the bells before light
counter = the counter | my counter

any
opinion @1 houses mixed 1 : The four Houses bow low in the Rain Hall, then bare their teeth back home. I'm frightened it comes to worse before spring. | All four Houses eat bread. That's all they've ever agreed on, and long may it last.
opinion @2 houses against 1 [world.times=hard|lean] : When the Houses squabble, the flour's dearer by the next sack. I've watched it twice. | Half the street short of supper, and the Houses squabbling yet over which of them sits nearest the fire.
opinion @1 merchants for 1 [self.role=merchant] : I keep #counter# myself. I could name what a cart of flour costs before it's past the gate. | Folk call a shopkeeper greedy. None of them has paid a miller in their lives.
opinion @1 drink against 1 : A cup undoes the knot on a tongue, and nobody ties it again by morning. | One cup at supper, fine. After the second it's promises, and nobody keeps them.
opinion @1 newcomers against 1 : New folk, fine. I'd still want to know whose stair they slept on before they come round my side. | A strange face on our street, and I lock #flourbin#. I'm sorry, but I do.
opinion @1 road against 2 : Past the walls? You couldn't pay me in gold. Not this year. | The road's where the flour comes from, and where the trouble comes from too.
opinion @2 road against 3 [world.wagons=raided] : A cart robbed again. That's flour I'll never get my paws in, and dearer bread by Marketday. | Robbed carts mean short loaves. I hate cutting them short.
opinion @1 sump against 1 : I don't go below Market Square after dark. Nor much before. I'm sorry for the ones who live there. | The Sump frightens me. That's not fair to the folk in it, I know.
opinion @1 watch for 1 : #watch.capitalize# go by my shutters two or three times between dusk and the bells. I rest easier hearing their boots. | No friends of mine, but #thief.s# give my till a wide berth while #watch# are about.
opinion @1 church for 1 : I like #chapel# best. The praying's fine, but what brings me back is the hush. | I reckon my whisper carries far enough for the Bright One. That's a mercy to anyone who's heard my singing.
opinion @1 city mixed 1 : It's a fine city when it isn't raining. So, now and then. | Nowhere else would have me, and I'd not go. I'd only like it a bit drier and a bit quieter.

grown
line @1 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. If it's bread, it's on the shelf. | #greet#. A new face. That's all right.
line @2 greet [self.working=yes self.job=baker] : #greet#. Flour on everything, me included. | #greet#. Mind the trays; they're hot.
line @3 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. Paws in dough since #bells#, me. | Morning. First batch is nearly out.
line @4 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. I'm half asleep, so speak up. | Evening. There's just a little warmth left in the bricks.
line @5 greet : #greet#. Don't mind the ink. It's always on me. | #greet#. I was just turning a riddle over.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. You caught me between batches. | #greet#. Quiet so far. That suits me.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : Oh. You. I'd hoped it would be. | You. Good. Find a stool that's not got flour on it.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Talk while I knead, if you don't mind. | Hello. Kneading needs paws, not ears. Talk away.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. You're out late. | Evening. I'll be in bed by the next bell.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : I saved you the crusty end. Don't tell anyone. | Sit down. I'll fret about you less if you sit.
line @1 again.open : Twice today. I don't mind it. | You again. I've a fresh riddle since.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. I've #dough# to see to. | I'll get back to it. #farewell#.
line @2 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Drop the bar on your door. I do. | Good night. My oven's lit before light, so that's me.
line @1 farewell.return : #farewell#. Keep dry. | Go carefully. And come for bread.
line @2 farewell.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Mind the step. | Good night, then. Sleep, if you can.
line @1 react.news : Oh. Tell me slowly. | Go on. Kneading's no reason not to listen.
line @1 react.news.new : That's not reached me. Things never reach me first. | That's new. I'll fret about it all night now.
line @1 react.news.known : Three customers told me that before noon. | I'd heard. Half the street had it before me.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Say it again tomorrow. If it hasn't changed, I'll believe it. | Hm. Folk say a lot over a loaf that isn't so.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : Oh, no. That's hard. I'll go and sit in #chapel# for them. | That's cruel. Every one of them, poor souls.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Oh, good. Someone'll be wanting a cake. | Good news. It's that rare I'll need a stool.
line @1 answer.how : Tired. Fine. Tired. | Well enough. Flour in my chest, but well enough.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : Busy. The loaves don't wait for anybody. | Up since #bells# and not sat down once.
line @2 answer.how [here.weather=rain|fog] : Damp. It settles on my chest. It's nothing. | Wet out, warm in. I've the oven.
line @1 ask.how : Are you eating? You look thin. | Sleeping? I'm not, much.
line @1 sympathise : Oh. That's a weight. Sit a while. | I'm sorry. I've no clever words for it. Only sorry.
line @1 reassure : It'll mend. Most things I've fretted over did. | Leave the fretting to me. I've the practice.
line @1 decline : I'd rather not, I'm sorry. The oven wants me back by the bell. | Not today. There's a batch proving and I daren't leave it to itself.
line @1 accept_decline : No, that's fair. Another day. | All right. I'll not press you.
line @1 accept_preclose : Go on, then. I've loaves to turn. | Yes. Go on. Watch the wet cobbles.
line @1 thank : Thank you. It comes out small, but I mean it big. | That's kind. There's a loaf in it for you.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It was nothing. A loaf's a loaf. | Don't. You'll make me blush through the flour.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes] : Back I go. The oven sulks without me. | That's the batch calling. I have to go.
line @2 preclose : Well. I should get on. | Off you go. My worries won't keep themselves.
line @1 tease.return : Oh. Was that a joke? I'll laugh later. | You're a wicked one. Go on, then.
line @1 ack : Mm. Right. | Yes, all right.
line @1 give.advice : Wrap it in paper and keep it off the floor. Rats. | Get something hot inside you before you face that weather.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : Keep inside the gate today. Please. | There's #bandits# about. Stay inside the walls.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Over here. I've a crust going spare. | Here. In, before you drown.
line @1 accept_apology : It's forgotten. I'd forgotten it already. | No, it's all right. I fret over less than that.
scene riddle_paper
when topic=work|pastimes self.job=baker
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / react.self
a : I write a #riddle# on every bread-paper. Folk bring the answer back with the next loaf. | There's ink on my paws from #paper#. A riddle on each one, every morning.
b : Do many get them right? | What sort of riddles?
a : About one in five. The rest buy another loaf and try again. | Small ones. The bakehouse, mostly. The oven, the flour, the cat.
b : One in five's not bad going. | A riddle round your bread. I'd like that.

scene rising_riddle
when topic=pastimes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / react.self
a : Here's this morning's. I rise without legs and I fall without a sound. | Here's one. I've teeth and no mouth, and I get the first of every loaf.
b : A cat getting to its feet? No. I give up. | A saw? No. A knife, it'd be.
a : Dough. You knock it back and down it goes. | The bread knife. You had it, near enough.
b : Dough. I'll remember that one. | Near enough counts for nothing, I suppose.

scene wet_riddle
when topic=pastimes|smalltalk
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / react.self
a : A new one. I'm wet every day I live, and the rain's never touched me. | I've a crust and no loaf, and I'm broken every morning in winter.
b : A fish. | The ice on the water butt.
a : Your tongue. Fish get rained on. | Yes. #oath#, you're quick.
b : Fair. I'll have the next one off you. | I've broken a few of those myself.

scene warm_bread
when topic=food
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Nothing beats bread #warm#. I'll argue it with anybody. | Every morning the first heel off the first loaf is mine. Standing up, by #oven#.
b : Better than a roast? | Every morning?
a : Better than a roast. A roast doesn't smell like that the whole length of a street. | Every morning. It's my one meal I stop for, and I don't even sit down for it.

scene haggle_morning
when topic=prices|work self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / assess
a : Somebody haggled me over one loaf today. One. Loaf. | I ink the price on the paper so nobody haggles. Folk try anyway.
b : What did you say? | Folk always will.
a : I said the same number twice, then stared at my paws till it was paid. | I hate it. My paws shake. I'd sooner give the loaf away.

scene flour_dear
when topic=prices|price_cause world.times=hard|lean self.job=baker
does tell.worry / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Flour's dear again. I don't know how I'll keep #loaves# the same size. | The miller's put the sacks up. I've not put the bread up. Not yet.
b : Could you make them smaller? | You'll have to, surely.
a : I could. I'd lie awake over every one. | Next week, maybe. I keep saying next week.

scene thunder_roof
when topic=weather here.weather=storm
does tell.feeling / react.self / assess
a : Hear it? I love #thunder#, when I'm under #roof#. | Thunder all night. I lay awake and enjoyed every roll of it.
b : It frightens me. | Didn't you sleep at all?
a : Noise, nothing more, and it can't reach me in here. I love that. | Not a wink. I'll be useless at the dough today, and I'm glad.

scene storm_oven
when topic=weather here.weather=storm self.job=baker
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : The oven roars back at the thunder. I like to think they're talking. | Storm's put the whole street indoors. Nobody's buying; everyone's listening.
b : What would they be saying? | Quiet day for you, then.
a : The oven says it's hotter. The sky says it's louder. Neither one gives in. | Quiet. I'll sell it all tomorrow, a day old, and feel bad about it.

scene damp_chest
when topic=health|weather here.weather=rain|fog
does tell.self / disagree / insist / ack
a : That's flour in my chest, nothing more. Stop looking at me. | The damp's in the bakehouse walls. I don't mind it. Much.
b : That's a damp cough, not flour. | That chest wants a healer, not more flour.
a : It's flour. I say it's flour, and the flour agrees with me. | I'll see somebody when it's dry. So, never.
b : Have it your way. Something hot for it, at least. | A dry day, then. I'll remember you said it.

scene chapel_quiet
when topic=faith
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I go to #chapel# when it's empty. Between the services, mid-afternoon. | I don't pray much out loud. I sit a bit. My silences reach the Bright One as easily as hymns.
b : Not for the service? | That's praying too, I'd say.
a : The service is lovely. It's the quiet after that I go for. | I hope it counts. Sitting's what I'm good at.

scene restday_paws
when topic=faith|day here.day=rest self.job=baker
does tell.self / give.advice / assess
a : Restday, and the oven cold. My paws keep kneading nothing. | No baking today. I'll go and sit in the chapel once the crowd's gone.
b : Rest them, for once. | When's that, usually?
a : I'll try. I'll end up writing riddles, I expect. | After the noon bell. I'll have it nearly to myself.

scene rat_trap
when topic=animals|home|work self.job=baker
does tell.worry / react.self / assess
a : I found #rats# in #flourbin# not long ago. One ear stays awake for it now. | I set traps every night and check them before #bells#. I hate it.
b : They're all over the lower streets. | Any luck with them?
a : I know. That's why the ear stays awake. | Two this month. I carried them out on a shovel with my eyes shut.

scene fret_kin
when topic=family self.father
does tell.worry / give.advice / assess
a : I fret about #self.father#. I fret about everything, but that one most. | I worry about #self.father# on the stairs. I'd not say so; I'd only get told off.
b : Somebody ought to fret, I suppose. | Say it anyway. Kin like being fretted over.
a : Somebody ought. I've a gift for it. | Maybe. I'll leave the lamp on the landing, and say nothing.

scene trusted_one
when topic=friends self.friend
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : If I had to leave my keys with anybody, it'd be #self.friend#. Only #self.friend#. | On a bad day I go and sit by #self.friend#. We don't talk.
b : That's no small thing, keys. | No talking at all?
a : No. I've only the one spare. | None. A spare answer would spoil the joke.

scene oven_mine
when topic=work self.hand self.working=yes self.job=baker
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd teach #self.hand# on a twist loaf before a plain one. | I'd offer #self.hand# the kneading board and keep the oven to myself.
b : Twists first? | Why the oven?
a : Two ropes of dough show up an uneven paw. A round loaf can hide one. | Because if it goes wrong, I want it to be my own fault.

scene stone_loaves
when topic=memories self.job=baker self.age>=20
does tell.memory / react.self / assess
a : The year the markets went over to food, I had to learn the oven in a month. My first loaves were stones. | My first batch, I cried over. Somebody bought one anyway, to be kind.
b : They're not stones now. | That's a kind customer.
a : No. It took a whole winter, but no. | Who it was, I never learned. I write the riddles a little for them.

scene before_light
when topic=day|work here.time=dawn self.job=baker
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : I'm up before #bells#. The street's black and the oven's the only light. | First light is mine. First batch out, and nobody wanting anything yet.
b : I couldn't do it. | And then?
a : The body learns. Or it learns to be tired, which is near enough. | Then the latch goes, and everybody wants everything at once.

scene answer_back
when topic=work|smalltalk self.job=baker
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : A pup brought back yesterday's answer chalked on a bit of slate. Right, too. | Somebody wrote a riddle back to me on the paper. I've not solved it.
b : Did the pup get a prize? | What's the riddle?
a : A loaf's heel. That's the prize, and a good one. | I'll not tell you. I want to get it first.

scene fog_door
when topic=weather here.weather=fog
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Three doors down's gone in this. I keep thinking someone's at the door. | Fog's in the bakehouse. The loaves come out tasting of it, I swear.
b : Nobody's out in it. | Tasting of fog?
a : I know. That's worse, somehow. | Damp. They taste damp. Nobody else can tell, but I can.

scene ink_runs
when topic=weather here.weather=rain self.job=baker
does tell.complaint / give.advice / assess
a : Rain again. The paper goes soft and the ink runs off the riddles. | Wet days, folk buy one loaf and stand by the oven an hour.
b : Can't you wrap them twice? | Can't blame them.
a : I could. Then it'd be two riddles, and I've only so many in me. | I don't. I like the company, so long as nobody talks too much.

scene dry_sky
when topic=weather here.weather=clear
does tell.self / give.advice / assess
a : No rain. There'll be a catch somewhere. | Sun! Every door on the street's open and squinting.
b : Take it while it's here. | It's nice to see.
a : I'll try, with one ear for the gutters. | It is. I'll go back in where it's warm, though.

scene houses_bow
when topic=times|smalltalk|gossip here.region=ridgemere
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : The Houses bow and mean none of it. Some year the bowing will stop. | Should the Houses quarrel in earnest, the likes of us go without bread first.
b : It always comes to words and stops there. | It hasn't, all my life.
a : I hope you're right. I'll lay in a sack of flour anyway. | All my life so far. That's the part I don't like.

scene new_on_street
when topic=newcomer
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / react.self
a : There's a new wolf somewhere near. I'll learn them by their loaf before I learn their face. | I hear there's somebody new. I'll lock #flourbin# tonight. I'm sorry, but I will.
b : What does bread tell you? | That's hard on them.
a : Rye folk are careful with coin. The white-loaf ones are spending somebody else's. | It is. I'll unlock it once I know them.
b : I'll mind what I buy, then. | Fair enough.

scene carts_late
when topic=caravan|prices world.wagons=late self.job=baker
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : No flour carts yet. I've a day's baking left. Two, maybe. | Carts late. I've measured what's in #flourbin# three times since breakfast.
b : They'll come. | Three times?
a : They will. I'll fret till they do. | Four, now I've said it. I'll go and look again.

scene cart_taken
when topic=caravan|bandits world.wagons=raided self.job=baker
does tell.worry / reassure / assess
a : Robbers had a cart past the walls. If it carried flour, it carried mine. | Word is the road's had another wagon emptied. I'll bake small loaves till we know.
b : Might not have been flour. | That's hard on folk.
a : Might not. I'll still lie awake over it. | I know. A small loaf still feeds somebody. I keep saying it to myself.

scene lean_shelf
when topic=times world.times=hard|lean
does tell.self / praise / deflect_praise
a : Times are lean. I keep yesterday's loaves back for whoever looks hungriest. | Fewer loaves go out every week. Folk buy half and make it last.
b : That's good of you. | Everybody's stretching.
a : Hush. You'll have me blushing into the dough. | We all are. I'm stretching the dough, if I'm honest.

scene cake_any_day
when topic=times world.times=good|boom
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Good times. The shelf's bare before noon, and the oven can't keep pace. | Folk are buying cake on an ordinary day. That's how you know.
b : That's no bad thing. | Cake on an ordinary day!
a : No. I'm too tired to enjoy it, that's all. | I know. I bake it and worry it won't last.

scene into_the_dough
when topic=gossip
does keep_confidence / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : All sorts gets said across #counter#. It stays in the bakehouse when I lock up. | Somebody told me something about #topic.subject# today. I'll not repeat it.
b : Not even to me? | Why not?
a : Not even you. It goes straight into the dough and gets baked. | In case it's false. Then I'd have carried it, and that's on me.

scene kind_word
when topic=gossip topic.claim_kind=marriage|newcomer|promise_kept
does tell.gossip / react.news / assess
a : I'll pass on one thing, because it's a nice one. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. | Word is #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That cheered me right up.
b : That's good news. | Something kind, for a change.
a : It is. If it's true, there'll be a cake in it. | It is. It doesn't happen often over my counter.

scene paid_clear
when topic=dreams self.job=baker
does tell.plan / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : I'd like the bakehouse paid off clear. No debt, no landlord. Just the oven and me. | Some day I'll ink a riddle no wolf in this city can crack. And I'll never say the answer.
b : And then? | Never?
a : Then I'd sleep. A whole day. Maybe two. | Never. It'll be buried with me, and I'll smile about it.

scene festival_door
when topic=festival self.job=baker
does tell.self / react.self / assess
a : Festival day. Everyone wants cake, all at once, this minute. | I watch the festival from the bakehouse door. The music reaches; the elbows don't.
b : You'll be run ragged. | Don't you join in?
a : I will. I'll sleep on the flour sacks tonight. | I join in by baking. That's my dancing.

scene winter_door
when topic=season|weather here.season=winter self.job=baker
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail
a : Winter's the best time to be a baker. Half the street finds a reason to stand by your door. | I fret about #oven# in the cold. A crack in winter and that's a week gone.
b : Warmest trade in the city. | Has it ever cracked?
a : It is. In summer it's the hottest too, but I'd keep it. | Not yet. I'm touching the bench as I say that.

story first_loaf [topic=memories self.job=baker self.age>=20]
  : My first loaf sold was really my second go. The first went to the birds.
  : Its buyer turned up again at my door next morning, and my heart went into my boots.
  ask : What business did that buyer have with me?
  b : To complain, surely. Was it?
  : Another one. Just the same. I never knew if that was kindness or poor taste.

story the_window [topic=weather|memories self.age>=15]
  : The first storm I remember stripped the slates off half our street.
  : Everyone else hid under the stairs. Me, I wrapped up in a blanket by the window and watched.
  ask : Were you ever the one at the window, or the one under the stairs?
  b[old] : Under the stairs, and glad of it. You were a brave pup.
  b : At the window, every time. Were you frightened?
  : Not once. No night since has beaten it.

story the_hard_one [topic=pastimes self.age>=18]
  : One riddle of mine went unsolved a whole season. I was so proud.
  : Then an old carter just in by the North Road read it, laughed, and called the answer down the queue.
  b : Oh, that must have stung. What was the riddle?
  : I'll not say. I've changed it since, and it's harder now.
  b : I'll be back for it, then.

story the_grey_rat [topic=animals self.job=baker]
  : There was a big grey rat that came to the bakehouse every night for a month.
  : I never caught it, though I set every trap I could buy.
  ask : Can you guess what did?
  b : A cat, I'd bet. Was it yours?
  : A tom off the roofs, asleep on the flour sacks one morning. No more rat. It's slept by the oven ever since.

story said_nothing [topic=faith self.age>=20]
  : The winter I was most frightened, I went to the chapel every day and lit nothing and said nothing.
  : On the last day an old priest took the bench beside me and kept quiet too, for an hour.
  b : A priest who knows when to hush. Did it help?
  : It did, somehow. Since then I've done the same for others.

rule @bark_loaves
when concept=bark self.working=yes self.job=baker
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Loaves out. Still warm. | Fresh batch. Mind, it's hot.

rule @bark_ink
when concept=bark self.job=baker
does aside
cooldown 8h
say The ink's gone again. I had it a moment ago. | There. That's a hard one today.

rule @bark_flour
when concept=bark here.weather=rain|fog
does aside
cooldown 9h
say Flour. That's all that is. Flour. | It's nothing. It's the damp in the walls.

rule @bark_traps
when concept=bark self.dislikes=rats here.time=night
does aside
cooldown 12h
say Please don't be the flour bin. Please. | Traps are set. Bright One, let them stay empty.

rule @bark_thunder
when concept=bark here.weather=storm
does aside
cooldown 6h
say There it goes. Lovely. | Go on, then. Louder.

rule @bark_price
when concept=bark self.role=merchant self.working=yes
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Price is inked on the paper. | Same price as yesterday. I promise.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. You'll want bread, I expect. | #greet#. Sorry, flour everywhere. What is it?
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #greet#, #topic.player#. I've a new riddle, if you've time. | #topic.player#. Good. I'd half hoped you'd come by.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : Late, and my oven's lit before light. Quickly, please. | Evening. I'm half asleep standing up.
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : You can say it from there. | Keep the counter between us, please.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.job=baker] : I bake. Bread, mostly, and a cake for a wedding. Every loaf goes out in paper, with a riddle on it. | #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Up before the bells, flour to the elbows.
dlg @2 resident.job : #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Busy paws fret less. Mine stay busy. | I work. I worry. I write riddles in between.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : Bare shelf before noon, most days. The afternoons feel strange. | Very well. Too well to sleep.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. I'm not complaining. Not out loud. | It pays for the flour and the rent. Just.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Badly. I bake the same and sell half. | Poorly. The nights are long over it.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : From #world.news_from#, by way of a customer: #world.news#. That's all of it I've got. | Word came in with the flour, from #world.news_from#: #world.news#.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : Every loaf goes out with talk of #world.event_words#. | It's #world.event_words#, all morning, over the counter.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : Robbers have emptied a wagon outside the gate. Don't you go out there. | A cart's gone to robbers. I'm baking small for now.
dlg @4 resident.news [world.death=recent] : A death on our street. I wrapped up bread for the household. A loaf's the only comfort my paws can make. | There's been a burying. The street's gone quiet.
dlg @5 resident.news [world.wedding=recent] : A wedding! Somebody'll want a cake, and I hope it's from me. | Someone's wed. The street's gone soft in the face over it.
dlg @6 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : #news_none# Flour came in. That's my news. | Nothing, and I'm glad. Quiet suits me.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : I don't usually. But folk are saying #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Leave me out of it, mind. | Over the counter I heard #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've told nobody else.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes topic.claim_kind=theft|wanted] : Folk say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. The till sleeps upstairs with me now. | It's said #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. Keep your purse close, please.
dlg @3 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : #gossip_none# Whatever I hear gets baked into the bread. | Nothing I'd pass on.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Mind you read the paper. | #farewell#. Come for bread.
dlg @2 resident.bye [here.time=dusk|night] : Good night. Bolt up tight. | Good night. I'm for my bed.
dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare self.job=baker] : #greet#. Loaves #warm#, and a riddle on every paper. | #greet#. Bread on the shelf, price on the paper. #shop_offer#
dlg @2 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. Did you get yesterday's riddle? | #greet#, #topic.player#. I kept a crusty one back, in case.
dlg @3 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : #shop_bare# I'm sorry. Come before the bells tomorrow. | Not a crumb left. I'm sorry. Early tomorrow.
dlg @4 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare] : #greet#. Have a look. Please don't haggle. | #greet#. Prices are on the paper. #shop_offer#
dlg @1 shop.prices : It's inked on the paper, and the ink doesn't bend. I'm sorry. | The flour went up, so the loaf went up. Not a farthing more than the flour did.
dlg @1 shop.bye : #shop_bye# Read the paper before you eat it. | #farewell#. Keep it wrapped. Rats.
graft resident.hub
choice What's the riddle on your bread-paper? [self.job=baker] -> paper
node paper
say Today's? I go in white and come out brown, and everybody's glad to see me burned.
mood pleased
free
choice A loaf of bread. -> paper_right
choice I give up. -> paper_tell
node paper_right
say #oath#, that's it. Nobody gets it that fast. Take a heel off the end; you've earned it.
choice Back to it. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye
node paper_tell
say A loaf. White in, brown out, and burned is wanted. Don't feel bad; most folk say toast.
choice Another time, then. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft shop.greet
choice Why won't you haggle? [self.role=merchant] -> nohaggle
node nohaggle
say Because my paws shake when folk do it, and then I give the loaf away. That's why the price is inked on: so neither of us suffers.
free
choice What if I can't pay it? -> nohaggle_poor
choice Fair enough. -> greet
node nohaggle_poor
say Then say so, plain, and take yesterday's loaf. That isn't haggling. That's only being hungry.
choice Thank you. -> greet
choice Goodbye. -> bye
